The replacement is a test artefact that sweeps a range of nominal sizes, in both internal and external features, in all three axes. Here is one in OpenSCAD; it is deliberately parametric so you can adapt the ranges to your work.
// accuracy_artefact.scad
// Print in the material and profile you actually use.
// Purpose: measure error as a FUNCTION OF NOMINAL SIZE, internal and external.
ext_sizes = [4, 6, 10, 16, 25]; // external square pillars (mm)
hole_sizes = [3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12]; // internal hole diameters (mm)
plate_t = 5; // plate thickness -> also a Z reference
pillar_h = 10; // pillar height -> another Z reference
pitch = 30; // grid spacing (mm)
margin = 10;
$fn = 180; // fine faceting: removes chord error
// so what remains is the PRINTER's error
plate_w = margin*2 + pitch * max(len(ext_sizes), len(hole_sizes));
plate_d = margin*2 + pitch * 2;
module plate() {
difference() {
cube([plate_w, plate_d, plate_t]);
// row of through-holes: the internal features
for (i = [0 : len(hole_sizes) - 1])
translate([margin + pitch*i + pitch/2, margin + pitch/2, -1])
cylinder(h = plate_t + 2, d = hole_sizes[i]);
}
}
module pillars() {
// row of square pillars: the external features
for (i = [0 : len(ext_sizes) - 1])
translate([margin + pitch*i + pitch/2 - ext_sizes[i]/2,
margin + pitch + pitch/2 - ext_sizes[i]/2,
plate_t])
cube([ext_sizes[i], ext_sizes[i], pillar_h]);
}
plate();
pillars();
// Z references you also measure:
// plate_t (5 mm) -> short Z dimension
// plate_t + pillar_h (15 mm) -> tall Z dimension
// plate_w, plate_d -> long X and long Y dimensions
Print it once. Then print it three times — repeatability is a separate question from accuracy and the artefact answers both.

The right tool is a printed gauge that sweeps clearance in known steps against a known reference. Use a real reference object rather than another printed part, so you are measuring one unknown instead of two: a ground dowel pin, a drill bit shank, an M3 screw, a 608 bearing, a specific connector.
// fit_gauge.scad — sweep clearance against a KNOWN reference part.
// Print, then test each hole against the real object. Record which fits how.
ref_d = 3.00; // measured diameter of the reference (mm)
clearances = [0.00, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50];
thick = 6; // engagement depth matters -> keep it realistic
pitch = 12;
$fn = 180;
difference() {
cube([pitch * len(clearances) + 10, 20, thick]);
for (i = [0 : len(clearances) - 1]) {
translate([10 + pitch*i, 10, -1])
cylinder(h = thick + 2, d = ref_d + clearances[i]);
// engrave the clearance value next to each hole
translate([6 + pitch*i, 2.5, thick - 0.6])
linear_extrude(1)
text(str(clearances[i]), size = 2.6);
}
}
